```dart
final unknown = [
 for (final key in node.keys)
   if (!{...}.contains('$key')) {
     '$key',
   },
];
``

Hmm, in Dart, `if (cond) { expr }` inside a collection literal — the braces there would be parsed as a set/map literal, NOT a block! `if (!set.contains('$key')) { '$key', }` — that's a set literal containing '$key'. Actually that would still produce the same result (a one-element set... no wait, it would add the SET to the list, making List<Set<String>>). That's wrong. Let me fix to the standard single-expression form:

```dart
final unknown = [
 for (final key in node.keys)
   if (!{'theme', 'classic', 'showCompactionDetails'}.contains('$key'))
     '$key',
];
``

The original code was `if (!{'theme'}.contains('$key')) '$key',`. I introduced braces — bug. Fix it.

